              "In 312, Constantine was gambling the empire against his rival Maxentius at the gates of Rome. According to his biographers, before the battle he saw a sign in the sky and was told «by this you will conquer»; he had the symbol of Christ painted on the shields, and he won.",
              "The following year he proclaimed freedom of worship for everyone and the return of confiscated property to the churches. It's worth being precise: Constantine did NOT make Christianity the official religion. He legalized it, protected it and funded it. The state religion would come nearly seventy years later, with Theodosius.",
              "Why did he do it? Probably out of conviction and politics at the same time. The Christians were a minority — perhaps 10% of the population — but they were urban, literate, hierarchically organized and with a loyalty proven under torture: as allies they were worth far more than their numbers. And one God in heaven fitted nicely with one emperor on earth.",
              "What he did afterward is what truly changed history: he exempted the clergy from taxes, gave bishops judicial powers, funded enormous basilicas, declared Sunday an official day of rest, founded a new Christian capital, Constantinople, and convened and presided over the Council of Nicaea. From that moment on, the Church and political power are bound together for fifteen hundred years.",
              "And he was no saint: he had his eldest son and his wife executed in circumstances never cleared up, and he was baptized only on his deathbed.",
