              "The gods of Olympus didn't vanish in a day or through a military defeat. They faded out over two centuries, and in that fading there was a bit of everything: loss of prestige, laws, money and violence.",
              "First, the wearing down. Classical religion was above all public ritual: sacrifices, processions, omens. It promised no personal salvation, gave no comfort in death, demanded no morality, had no sacred books and no support network. In an empire in crisis, a great many people were looking for exactly that.",
              "Second, the money. As soon as the emperors were Christian, the temples stopped receiving public funds and the priesthoods stopped being good business and a route to social advancement. Being a Christian started to be professionally convenient, and the elite changed religion with notable speed.",
              "Third, an attempt to turn back that failed. The emperor Julian tried to restore paganism by copying from the Christians what worked: an organized hierarchy, schools and, above all, charitable works. He wrote that it was «a disgrace» that it was the Christians and not the temples who fed the poor. He died on campaign two years later and his reform died with him.",
              "Fourth, the law. Theodosius banned sacrifices and worship in the temples, even at home. In 394 the sacred fire of Vesta, which had burned for more than a thousand years, went out, and the Olympic Games, which were a religious festival, stopped being held around that time.",
              "And fifth, violence. Crowds and monks destroyed temples and statues; in 391 the Serapeum of Alexandria, one of the most magnificent sanctuaries of the ancient world, was razed, and in 415 the philosopher and mathematician Hypatia was lynched in that same city.",
              "In the end, much of it wasn't destroyed: it was reused. Temples turned into churches (the Pantheon in Rome has been a church since the year 609, which is why it's intact), old festivals turned into Christian feasts, local gods turned into protecting saints. Even the name: «pagan» comes from paganus, «of the countryside», because the last to stick with the old gods were the country people.",
