          "When the empire split in two, the western half came apart and the eastern one kept working for another thousand years, with its capital at Constantinople. We call that the Byzantine Empire today, but it's a name historians invented in the 16th century: they called themselves «Romans» until the very last day.",
          "It survived because it had what the West had lost: the rich part of the empire (Egypt, Syria, Asia Minor), control of the trade between Europe and Asia, a stable gold currency — the Byzantine solidus was the reliable money of the Mediterranean for seven hundred years — a professional bureaucracy… and the most formidable walls in the world.",
