            cuerpo: [
              "They were 90% of the population, so talking about the Middle Ages means talking about them, not about the knights.",
              "THEIR HOUSE. A single room of wood, mud and straw, with a floor of packed earth, no chimney (the smoke went out through a hole or the door, and the walls were black), no glass in the windows, a fire in the middle, a straw pallet shared by the whole family and the animals indoors in winter, because they gave off heat. Fleas, lice and smoke, always.",
              "THEIR FOOD. Dark rye or barley bread, between one and two kilos a day, which was the base of almost all their calories. A pottage of peas, broad beans, cabbage and onion, cooked in the same pot for days. Cheese, eggs, a little salted fish. Weak beer or watered wine, because river water was dangerous. Meat, very little and almost always salt pork or bacon: the big animals belonged to the lord, and hunting in his woods could cost you a hand.",
              "THEIR WORK. Sunrise to sunset, to the rhythms of the land. And on top of that, compulsory work for the lord: two or three days a week on his fields, plus whatever was needed at harvest or vintage, unpaid. There were also days to be given to repairing the castle, the roads or the bridges.",
              "WHAT THEY PAID. Rent to the lord (in grain, eggs, hens); the tithe to the Church (a tenth of everything); and a toll on almost anything: for grinding grain in the lord's mill, for baking bread in his oven, for pressing grapes in his winepress, for crossing his bridge, for selling at his market. Owning your own mill was forbidden.",
              "THEIR FREEDOM. Serfs couldn't leave the land, or marry outside the manor without paying a fee, or inherit without paying another — sometimes the best beast in the house — when the father died. The lord was also the judge: he fined, he flogged, he put people in the stocks.",
              "THEIR HEALTH. Life expectancy at birth was around 30–35 years, but that figure is misleading: it was dragged down by infant mortality, which took one child in three before the age of five. Anyone who reached twenty had a reasonable chance of reaching fifty or sixty. Every few years there was hunger; the Great Famine of 1315–1317 killed millions in northern Europe.",
              "THE GOOD PART, BECAUSE THERE WAS ONE. There were between 80 and 100 feast days a year on which the Church forbade work, and they were made the most of: markets, pilgrimages, weddings, dances, ball games, music. The village community really worked: the common pastures were shared out, and people helped with the harvests and in misfortune.",
              "AND WHEN THEY'D HAD ENOUGH. They rebelled. The French Jacquerie in 1358; the great English revolt of 1381, with Wat Tyler and the preacher John Ball, who summed up the whole problem in one line: «when Adam delved and Eve span, who was then the gentleman?»",
            ],
            dato: "Fun fact: the droit du seigneur, that story about the lord taking the first night with the brides of his fief, appears in no serious medieval document. It's a later invention, very useful for shocking people. The real abuses were other ones: economic, judicial and everyday.",
