          "The churches of Rome and of Constantinople had been drifting apart for centuries without meaning to. It wasn't a divorce, it was a drift.",
          "They spoke different languages — Latin in the West, Greek in the East — and that meant they argued about the same texts without understanding each other. They lived in opposite political worlds: in the East there was a powerful emperor and the Church was folded into the state; in the West there was no real emperor and the pope had filled that vacuum, politically as well.",
