          "In 1095, Pope Urban II gave a speech at Clermont that set two centuries of war in motion. He asked the Christians of Europe to go and free Jerusalem, in Muslim hands for four centuries, and he offered something decisive: the remission of sins to anyone who died on the way. The crowd answered by shouting «God wills it».",
          "The motives were a mixture: sincere faith and fear of hell (which in that frame of mind was very real), the chance of getting land and loot, escaping debts or a sentence, the desire for adventure, and the Church's interest in channeling outward the violence of a warrior nobility that spent its life killing itself.",
