          "In October 1347, some Genoese ships coming from the Black Sea docked at Messina, in Sicily. They brought the disease with them. Within five years it had crossed the whole of Europe, as far as Scandinavia and Russia.",
          "The cause was a bacterium, Yersinia pestis, which traveled in the fleas of rats and also in human fleas and lice, and which in its pulmonary form passed directly from one person to another through breathing. Nobody knew any of that: people talked about corrupted air, about planetary conjunctions, about divine punishment.",
          "The symptoms were brutal and extremely fast: high fever, painful blackish lumps in the neck, the armpits or the groin, dark blotches on the skin, and death in two to seven days. Untreated, between 60% and 90% of the infected died; the pulmonary form was practically always fatal.",
          "Between a third and half of the European population died: probably 25 million people in Europe and perhaps twice that in Asia and the Near East. In many cities there weren't enough living to bury the dead, and the bodies piled up in mass graves.",
          "Fear produced what it usually produces: someone to blame was sought. The Jews were accused of poisoning the wells and there were massacres and burnings in dozens of German and French cities — in Strasbourg, in 1349, hundreds of people were burned alive — even though Pope Clement VI expressly condemned those accusations. Many surviving communities emigrated eastward, above all to Poland.",
