          "Johannes Gutenberg, a goldsmith from Mainz, didn't invent «printing»: printing from woodblocks had been done in China for centuries. What he invented was a complete system for producing separate metal letters, all exactly the same height, reusable and combinable — plus the ink and the press to use them with.",
          "The numbers behind the change are brutal: in 1450 there wasn't a single printing press in Europe; by 1500 there were workshops in some 250 cities and around eight or nine million books had been printed. The price of a book fell so far that, for the first time, an artisan or a student could come to own one.",
          "And everything else followed. Luther's Reformation was the first media event in history: his texts spread across Germany in weeks. The Scientific Revolution was possible because an astronomer in Prague could see exactly the same diagram, with no copyist's errors, as an astronomer in Seville. Spellings and national languages were fixed. Newspapers, indexes, numbered pages and intellectual property were born.",
          "The bad multiplied too: one of the great bestsellers of those years was the Malleus Maleficarum, the manual for hunting witches. The printing press doesn't make people better: it makes bigger and faster everything people already want to say.",
