          "For centuries, a statement about nature was accepted in Europe for two reasons: because a prestigious ancient author had said it (Aristotle, Galen, Ptolemy) or because it fitted religious doctrine. The method was to read, interpret and argue, not to check.",
          "And it worked terribly. It was believed that heavy objects fall faster, that the heart warms the blood, that flies are born out of rotten meat and that the Sun goes round the Earth. Nobody checked, because checking wasn't considered necessary.",
          "The change was to invert the hierarchy: if an authority and an experiment contradict each other, the experiment wins. It sounds obvious today; at the time it was close to insolence.",
          "The pieces arrived from different places. Copernicus proposed that the Earth goes round the Sun. Vesalius opened corpses and showed that Galen had got dozens of details wrong. Kepler discovered that the orbits are ellipses because the data didn't fit circles. Galileo measured, timed and actually looked through a telescope. Bacon argued that you have to experiment systematically and in an organized way. Descartes demanded doubting everything and building from scratch. Boyle published his experiments in such detail that anyone could repeat them. And Newton pulled it all together into a mathematical physics.",
          "That's the Scientific Revolution: not a discovery, but the invention of a method for discovering and, above all, for detecting mistakes. It's the tool that produced everything else: modern medicine, electricity, computers, vaccines.",
