          "The middle leg, the crossing, lasted between one and three months in conditions that are hard to describe: men and women chained in 'tween decks less than a meter high, lying down unable to sit up, with dysentery, without air and without enough water. Between 10% and 20% died on the way, and sometimes far more.",
          "The figures are enormous: around twelve and a half million Africans were put on ships between the 16th and 19th centuries, and close to eleven million arrived alive. Most didn't go to the United States, as people tend to think, but to Brazil and the Caribbean, to the sugar plantations, where the life expectancy of a newly arrived slave was counted in a few years.",
          "Sugar is the economic key to all of this: it was an addictive product, consumed in ever greater quantities and with a very high profit, and its plantations needed a great deal of cheap, disposable labor. The ports that grew on this trade — Liverpool, Bristol, Nantes, Bordeaux, Lisbon, Cádiz — funded banks, insurance, shipyards and a good part of the capital that later fed the Industrial Revolution.",
