          "The Enlightenment was the idea that reason, and not tradition or revelation, should guide how society is organized. Its motto, according to Kant, was «dare to think for yourself».",
          "Applied to politics, it was dynamite. If power comes not from God but from an agreement between people, then a king doesn't rule by divine right, but because the governed accept him — and if he fails to keep his side, he can be replaced.",
          "Each thinker contributed a piece. Locke: there are natural rights — life, liberty, property — that come before any government. Montesquieu: to stop power abusing, it has to be divided into three — legislative, executive and judicial — and made to watch over each other. Rousseau: sovereignty belongs to the people. Voltaire: freedom of expression and religious tolerance, with a tireless campaign against the judicial abuses of his time. Beccaria: against torture and the death penalty. Adam Smith: wealth comes from work and from exchange. And Olympe de Gouges and Mary Wollstonecraft pointed out what the rest were overlooking: that those «universal» rights were being written for men only.",
          "Their great tool was Diderot and d'Alembert's Encyclopédie: 28 volumes gathering all useful knowledge, the manual trades included, with the declared intention of «changing the common way of thinking». It spread in spite of the censorship, and a good part of the nobility and the enlightened clergy bought it.",
