          "Throughout all of history, the force available for work had always been the same: people, animals, running water and wind. All of them have limits and all of them depend on the place and the weather: a mill needs a river, a ship needs wind.",
          "Steam broke that dependence. Earlier machines already existed — Newcomen's, used for draining mines — but they devoured coal and their efficiency was dreadful, because in each cycle the same cylinder had to be heated and cooled.",
          "James Watt saw the exact problem and solved it in 1769 with a simple, brilliant idea: separate the condenser from the cylinder, so the cylinder stayed hot all the time. With that, coal consumption fell dramatically. Then he added rotary motion, and that was what made it possible to drive not just pumps but factory machinery, wheels, propellers and shafts.",
