              "Between September 1793 and July 1794, the Republic defended itself through terror organized by law. The context matters for understanding it, though it doesn't justify it: France was invaded by Austria, Prussia, Great Britain and Spain all at once, with a civil war in the Vendée, revolts in Lyon, Marseille and Toulon, hunger in the cities and the popular leader Marat murdered in his bath.",
              "Real power passed to a committee of twelve men, the Committee of Public Safety, with dictatorial powers. Its central figure was Maximilien Robespierre, a provincial lawyer nicknamed «the Incorruptible»: austere, personally honest, he lived in a carpenter's house, and years earlier he had argued in the Assembly for the abolition of the death penalty.",
              "His logic was set down in a speech: «terror is nothing other than justice, prompt, severe, inflexible… virtue without terror is powerless». When someone becomes convinced they represent virtue, any opposition turns into treason.",
              "The overall figures: some 16,600 official death sentences across France, plus between ten and twelve thousand people executed or killed without trial, and around 300,000 arrested. And separately, the repression of the Vendée, with the «infernal columns» razing villages, the mass shootings in Lyon and the collective drownings in the Loire ordered by the deputy Carrier in Nantes: the dead there are counted at between 170,000 and 250,000.",
              "The king and the queen fell, the Girondins, Madame Roland — who left at the foot of the guillotine the line «oh liberty, what crimes are committed in your name» — Olympe de Gouges, for having written a declaration of the rights of woman, and Lavoisier, the founder of modern chemistry, of whom it was said that «it took an instant to cut off that head, and perhaps a hundred years will not produce another like it». And the revolutionaries themselves fell: Danton and Desmoulins, Robespierre's old comrades, were guillotined in April 1794 for asking for clemency. Danton, on his way to the scaffold, asked that they show his head to the people: «it's worth seeing».",
              "And one figure that breaks the cliché: most of those guillotined weren't aristocrats. Around 85% were commoners — peasants, day laborers, artisans, shopkeepers — accused of hoarding grain, of deserting, of talking too much or of having ended up on the wrong list. Nobles were less than 10%.",
              "The end came when the Terror started to threaten the deputies themselves. On 9 Thermidor (27 July 1794), Robespierre was shouted down in the Convention, he was prevented from speaking — someone cried that «the blood of Danton is choking him» — and he was arrested. During the arrest a shot shattered his jaw. He spent his last night stretched out on a table, unable to speak, and the next day he was guillotined with twenty-one followers; the executioner tore the bandage off before the blade and his scream was heard across the square.",
              "And a question was left open that's worth thinking about: was the Terror the price of saving the Republic from the invader and from civil war, or the moment when a revolution made for liberty started devouring its own people? Almost all the great political debates since then about the «state of emergency», security and rights are children of those eleven months.",
