          "Throughout all of history, a person's maximum speed had been that of a horse at a gallop, and only for a short while. A trip from Madrid to Barcelona was a matter of weeks.",
          "The railway broke that ceiling. It combined three things: the steam engine, iron rails (which cut friction) and a business organization capable of building entire lines. The first passenger line with a locomotive, Liverpool–Manchester, opened in 1830, and within fifty years there were hundreds of thousands of kilometers of track in the world.",
          "It changed the human landscape: cities grew around the stations, suburbs appeared (you could live far away and work in the center), markets stopped being local, and fresh fish, milk and fruit reached places where they had never been seen.",
          "And it changed time, literally. Before, every city had its own hour, set by its own sun. With trains crossing a country in a day, timetables were impossible to coordinate, so a common official time was imposed and, in 1884, world time zones. The time on your phone is an invention of the railway.",
