          "Before Darwin, the accepted explanation was that each species had been created just as it is. But there were facts that didn't fit: the fossils of animals that no longer exist, the similar species on neighboring islands, the useless organs (what does a whale want with finger bones in its flippers?).",
          "Darwin spent five years going around the world on the Beagle observing and collecting samples, and then more than twenty years piling up evidence without publishing, aware of the storm he was going to cause. He published in 1859, pushed into it partly because another naturalist, Alfred Russel Wallace, had reached the same idea on his own.",
          "The idea is astonishingly simple and has three ingredients: the individuals of a species aren't identical; some of those differences are inherited; and more individuals are born than can survive. The consequence is inevitable: those who happen to have some variation that fits their surroundings better leave more offspring, and that variation becomes more common. Repeated over millions of years, it produces new species.",
          "What matters is what the theory does NOT say. It doesn't say that «the strongest» survives, but the one that fits its particular surroundings best — sometimes the smallest, the most inconspicuous or the one that cooperates best. It doesn't say evolution has a goal or a direction toward the «higher». And it doesn't say that man comes from the monkey: it says that today's monkeys and we share a common ancestor.",
