            titulo: "The black soldiers: 180,000 men who earned a country",
            cuerpo: [
              "When the war began, the Union army wouldn't accept black soldiers. The usual things were said about them: that they'd be no use, that they wouldn't hold under fire, that their presence would offend white men. Frederick Douglass, who had been born a slave and had become the most influential orator in the country, had been insisting on the opposite for years, and his argument was political: «let the black man get upon his person the brass letters U.S. … and there is no power on earth which can deny that he has earned the right to citizenship».",
              "In 1863, with the Emancipation Proclamation, recruitment was authorized and a dedicated office was created to organize the regiments of United States Colored Troops. By the end of the war around 180,000 black soldiers had served — close to 10% of the Union army — plus some 19,000 in the Navy. Many were men who had only just been freed and enlisted weeks later.",
              "And they were paid less: ten dollars a month with three deducted for the uniform, against thirteen for a white soldier. The 54th Massachusetts, one of the most famous black regiments, refused to accept the unequal pay and served eighteen months without taking a cent until Congress equalized the wages in 1864, retroactively. That same regiment stormed Fort Wagner in July 1863 in a suicidal attack; its white colonel, Robert Gould Shaw, died there, and Sergeant William Carney, wounded several times, saved the flag and became the first African American awarded the Medal of Honor.",
              "The Confederates didn't treat them as soldiers: official policy was to execute or enslave them, along with their white officers. At Fort Pillow, in April 1864, hundreds of black soldiers were murdered after surrendering. That hardened the war and forced Lincoln to threaten reprisals.",
              "Lincoln himself made clear what their participation had meant. In 1864 he wrote that without those more than a hundred thousand black soldiers, sailors and laborers, the Union would have to abandon the war «in three weeks». Which is to say: it wasn't a symbolic gesture, it was a decisive factor in the outcome.",
              "In the end there was some poetic justice too: some of the first regiments to enter Charleston, the cradle of secession, and Richmond, the Confederate capital, were black regiments, marching through the enslaved population that had just been freed.",
              "And then came the bitter part. Abolition was followed by the Fourteenth Amendment (citizenship and equal protection under the law, 1868) and the Fifteenth (the right to vote, 1870), and for a few years there were black congressmen, senators and judges in the south. But in 1877 the federal government withdrew the troops, and from then on came the segregation laws, the obstacles to voting and the terror of the Ku Klux Klan. It took another ninety years, and the civil rights movement of the 1960s, to achieve in practice what those amendments already said in writing.",
            ],
            dato: "Fun fact: Frederick Douglass, who had learned to read in secret as a slave because his master said reading «ruined» a slave, ended up being received at the White House by Lincoln and sending two of his sons to fight in the 54th Massachusetts.",
