          "The first Industrial Revolution was made by artisans and practical engineers, with almost no theory: they improved machines by intuition and trial. The second was different: it was built on chemistry, physics and electricity studied in laboratories and universities.",
          "The materials and the energy sources changed. Cheap steel (with the Bessemer and Siemens-Martin processes) made skyscrapers, enormous bridges, long-lasting rails and giant ships possible. Oil and electricity progressively replaced coal and steam. And chemistry created products that didn't exist in nature: synthetic dyes, fertilizers, explosives, primitive plastics, medicines like aspirin.",
          "The organization changed too. Along came the great corporations with thousands of employees, assembly-line production, professional management, mass advertising, department stores, brands and mail-order selling. And company-funded research laboratories: inventing stopped being a matter of solitary geniuses and became a department.",
          "One single discovery from this period deserves singling out above all the rest: Haber and Bosch's synthesis of ammonia (1909–1913), which made it possible to manufacture nitrogen fertilizers on an industrial scale. It's reckoned that today around half the world's population is fed thanks to that process. The same finding also served to manufacture explosives at scale, and its author took part in developing war gases.",
