          "In June 1914, a Serbian nationalist assassinated the heir to the Austro-Hungarian Empire in Sarajevo. Five weeks later, half of Europe was at war. That was the spark; the charge had been piling up for thirty years.",
          "There were four ingredients. A system of automatic alliances that turned any local conflict into a general one. An arms race and rigid military plans that, once mobilization began, couldn't be stopped. Nationalism and revanchism, above all between France and Germany. And colonial and industrial competition between powers that felt entitled to command.",
          "Everyone thought it would be a short war of movement, like those of the 19th century. It lasted four years and became something nobody had foreseen: trench warfare. Two parallel lines of ditches from the North Sea to Switzerland, sometimes separated by two hundred meters of mud, wire and corpses, where an offensive lasting months managed to advance a few kilometers and cost hundreds of thousands of lives.",
          "The technical reason is simple and brutal: defensive power — machine guns, quick-firing artillery, barbed wire — had far outstripped the means of attack, which were still men running. It took the generals years to accept it, and in the meantime battles like the Somme or Verdun were launched, with hundreds of thousands dead in each.",
          "Between nine and ten million soldiers and some seven million civilians died, and immediately afterward the flu pandemic of 1918 killed tens of millions more, helped along by the movements of troops.",
