          "How a totalitarian dictatorship comes to power in countries that hold elections is one of the most important questions in modern history, because they didn't arrive by invasion: they arrived by making use of the rules of the system they then destroyed.",
          "The ground was prepared by the First World War and its consequences: millions of resentful ex-combatants, the sense in Italy and Germany of having been humiliated in the treaties, the inflation that ruined the German middle class, the Great Depression with six million unemployed, the fear of communism after the Russian revolution and democratic parties incapable of forming stable governments.",
          "The method was similar in both cases. Mussolini took power in 1922 after years of violence by his squads against trade unionists and socialists, and once inside he gradually eliminated the opposition, the free press and elections. Hitler, after a failed coup in 1923, changed strategy and decided to get there through the ballot box: his party won the most votes in 1932 and in January 1933 he was appointed chancellor by the president. Weeks later, making use of the Reichstag fire, he suspended civil liberties and obtained full powers.",
          "Their common ingredients: an infallible leader; an internal enemy to blame for everything; the exaltation of the nation and of a glorious past; contempt for democracy and debate as weaknesses; paramilitary violence as a normal political tool; total control of the press, the school and the culture; the militarization of everyday life; and a modern, highly effective propaganda, with radio, film and mass rallies designed as spectacles.",
